    "reasoning": "All 16 selected answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-73620: leading nonzero entries lie in columns 1, 2, and 4, leaving columns 3 and 5. q-130954: pivots lie in columns 2, 3, and 4, leaving column 1. q-69950: expansion at entry (3,4) gives coefficient 2(-1)^7=-2; the minor is -7, so the determinant is 14. q-130030: expansion along row 4 gives coefficients -2 and -1; the minors are -20 and 18, yielding 22. q-77487: first-row expansion gives two minors, -47 and 58, whose sum is 11; the source's -0 equals 0. q-42095: third-row expansion gives 2(3)-(-6)=12. q-42104: fourth-column expansion gives coefficients 2 and -1; the minors are 68 and 37, yielding 99. q-77501: third-column expansion gives coefficients \\sqrt{3} and -\\sqrt{5}; the minors are -18 and -3, yielding -18\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{5}. q-42885: triangular diagonal products give det(A)=2 and det(B^T)=det(B)=-6, so the difference is 8. q-69972: det(-2I_4)=(-2)^4=16. q-42472: expansion along column 3 leaves a triangular minor with determinant 3(1)(-3)=-9. q-69982: expansion along column 4 gives -5 times a triangular minor of determinant 6, yielding -30. q-158108: the triangular column matrix has determinant 7(3)(-1)(5)=-105, so its volume is 105. Its worked solution incorrectly places ordinary matrix brackets in a scalar determinant equality, but the final determinant and volume are correct. q-158371: successive sparse expansions give determinant 8((-2)(7)-10(-3))=128, hence volume 128. q-42876: det(A)=det(A^T)=27 and det(I)=1, giving statement left sides 108, 21, and 54; only I and III hold. q-77504: det(A)=5 and det(\\sqrt{2}I_4)=4, giving statement left sides 1, -6, and -3; only I holds. Evidence comes from the captured worked solutions; no submitted grades or earlier answers were supplied.",
